  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><p tabindex=\"-1\">Delacroix’s Entry of the Crusaders into Constantinople Restored to Its Original Glory </p><div tabindex=\"-1\" role=\"main\" id=\"main-content\"><div id=\"page-article-block-0\"><div><p><a href=\"https://www.louvre.fr/decouvrir/vie-du-musee?type=750\"><span>Collection news</span></a><span>Restoration</span></p><div><picture><source media=\"(min-width: 820px)\" srcset=\"https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w739_h417_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 739w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w796_h449_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 796w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w846_h477_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 846w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w910_h513_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 910w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w952_h537_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 952w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1006_h568_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1006w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1059_h598_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1059w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1248_h704_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1248w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1478_h834_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1478w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1592_h898_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1592w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1691_h954_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1691w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1819_h1026_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1819w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1872_h1056_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1872w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1904_h1074_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1904w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w2011_h1135_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 2011w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w2118_h1195_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 2118w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1760px) 1058.67px, (min-width: 1200px) calc((100vw - 136px) * 0.67 - 24px), calc((100vw - 64px) * 0.67 - 16px)\"><source srcset=\"https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w656_h370_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 656w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w686_h387_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 686w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w722_h407_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 722w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w764_h431_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 764w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w792_h447_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 792w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w984_h555_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 984w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1029_h581_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1029w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1083_h611_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1083w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1146_h647_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1146w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1188_h670_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1188w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1376_h776_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1376w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w1460_h824_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 1460w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w2064_h1165_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 2064w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w2190_h1236_c0/public/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg 2190w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 740px) calc(100vw - 80px), calc(100vw - 32px)\"><img src=\"https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2026-05/la-prise-de-constantinople-par-les-croises-de-delacroix.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"3688\" height=\"2081\"></picture></div><div><p><span lang=\"EN\">Following a meticulous course of conservation treatment, Eugène Delacroix’s </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">Entry of the Crusaders&nbsp;into Constantinople</span></em><span lang=\"EN\"> is returning to the Red Rooms, joining a number of other famous works by the artist. By restoring the piece’s colours to their original brilliance, once darkened by yellowed varnish, the conservators’ work has allowed its complex significance to shine through once more.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p></div></div><p><span lang=\"EN\">Commissioned in 1838 by King Louis-Philippe&nbsp;I, the </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">Entry of the Crusaders into Constantinople (12&nbsp;April&nbsp;1204)</span></em><span lang=\"EN\"> was one of many artworks intended to decorate the historical galleries of the Château de Versailles. The painting was completed in 1840 and displayed at the 1841 Paris Salon, then found a more permanent home in the neo-Gothic Crusades Rooms in Versailles. Counted as one of Delacroix’s masterpieces, it returned to Paris on several occasions, including the 1855 and 1864 retrospective exhibitions dedicated to the artist. By virtue of its artistic merit, the Administration of Fine Arts decided to transfer ownership to the Louvre in 1881, replacing it with a full-scale copy at the Château de Versailles.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><div><!--?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?--><p><span lang=\"EN\">The </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">Entry of the Crusaders</span></em><span lang=\"EN\"> </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">into Constantinople</span></em><span lang=\"EN\"> </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">(12&nbsp;April&nbsp;1204)&nbsp;</span></em><span lang=\"EN\">was one of the major works painted by Delacroix at the height of his career. The mandated subject was particularly violent and ambiguous: the artist fulfilled the order with a masterful example of history painting. He represented the sack of the capital of the Eastern Christian empire by Western Christian knights, who, diverting away from their initial objective of retaking the Holy City from Muslim control, took advantage of the political instability and financial debt in which the Byzantine Empire was mired.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><p><span lang=\"EN\">Over the years, the painting’s old varnishes yellowed, darkening the picture and obscuring Delacroix’s chromatic effects. Conservation treatment undertaken between May&nbsp;2025 and April&nbsp;2026 reinforced the structure of the work&nbsp;– the canvas was mounted back onto its restored stretcher&nbsp;– and removed timeworn varnishes and materials left behind from previous restoration efforts from the pictorial layer. A new, transparent varnish was then applied, and the small gaps in the paint were filled in.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p>\n</div><p><span lang=\"EN\">Scientific imaging revealed how the artist made every effort to exhaustively depict the violence of this historical event. Delacroix’s first concept included the corpse of a soldier, trampled by a horse, in the foreground; he then chose to hide it behind banners. This discovery explains the sharp movement of the horse’s head, and its terrified gaze staring directly at the viewer. The animal expresses the compassion that the victors are unable to feel towards their victims.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><p><span lang=\"EN\">Delacroix also chose to highlight the plight of the enslaved women: shown in the foreground, they are emphasised by a vibrant colour scheme that makes them stand out from the other characters. By removing the dark screen of varnish that concealed the painting, the conservation treatment brought the colours much closer to the artist’s intended effect. His technical mastery now unveiled, the viewer can admire how he used an interweaving of colours, called </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">flochetage</span></em><span lang=\"EN\">, to give flesh tones an unprecedented vitality: the blues, purples, pinks, oranges, pale greens and warm greys meld together to render the captive woman’s nudity. The conservators’ work also restored the piece’s full depth of field, which is produced through various chromatic effects. In the background, white cities seem suspended in the emerald green of the mountains, which plunge down into turquoise-blue waters&nbsp;– they recall the watercolours of the Moroccan coast that the artist produced in 1832.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><div><!--?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?--><p><span lang=\"EN\">This substantial course of conservation treatment beautifully caps off a years-long conservation campaign of Delacroix’s large formats, which started in 2019.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><p><span lang=\"EN\">The preliminary studies and the conservation of </span><em><span lang=\"EN\">Entry of the Crusaders into Constantinople</span></em><span lang=\"EN\"> were performed thanks to the generous support of Ms Isabelle Ealet-Corbani.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p><p><strong>By Mathilde Dillmann</strong></p>\n</div></div><section id=\"you-may-also-like\"><p><h2><span>You may also like</span></h2></p><div><div><div><picture><source media=\"(min-width: 820px)\" srcset=\"https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w358_h224_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 358w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w386_h241_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 386w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w411_h257_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 411w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w443_h277_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 443w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w464_h290_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 464w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w491_h307_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 491w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w518_h324_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 518w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w608_h380_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 608w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w715_h447_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 715w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w772_h483_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 772w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w822_h514_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 822w, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/styles/w886_h554_c1/public/2026-02/affiche-louvre-x-snapchat.jpg 886w, https://api-